<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Novilara stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Novilara stele is an inscribed sandstone slab found in the 19th century at Novilara, near Pesaro in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its text, written in a variant of the Etruscan-derived alphabet, is in a language known as North Picene, which remains undeciphered and may not be Indo-European. The stele is also carved with scenes of ships, warriors and animals.</p></div>
